WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:09.800
<v Jussi Korhonen>Hello, this minicode is about threat modeling, and why you should do it before writing code.

2
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:20.400
<v Jussi Korhonen>Threat modeling answers four questions: what are we building, what can go wrong, what are we going to do about it, and did we do a good job.

3
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:32.700
<v Jussi Korhonen>We start from a data flow diagram. Draw the users, the services, the data stores and especially the trust boundaries.

4
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:46.900
<v Jussi Korhonen>Then go through STRIDE for each element: spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege.

5
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:55.200
<v Pekka Mäkelä>Do you use the Microsoft Threat Modeling Tool or just a whiteboard?

6
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:08.300
<v Jussi Korhonen>Mostly a whiteboard or Miro with the customer. The tool is nice for documentation but the conversation is what matters.

7
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:22.600
<v Jussi Korhonen>Example from a recent project: the Function App used a storage connection string. Threat: key leaks from a pipeline log. Mitigation: managed identity and disable shared keys.

8
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:36.800
<v Jussi Korhonen>Every threat becomes a backlog item with an owner. Otherwise the model is just a nice picture.

9
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:49.900
<v Jussi Korhonen>Revisit the model when the architecture changes, for example when you add a new integration or open a public endpoint.

10
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:58.100
<v Aino Virtanen>How long does a session usually take?

11
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:10.200
<v Jussi Korhonen>For a typical project two hours is enough for the first round. Thanks for listening, and do try it in your next project!
